# Web security (FastAPI + OWASP)

Sources: [FastAPI security](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/security/), [Starlette Security](https://www.starlette.dev/), [OWASP ASVS](https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/), [OWASP API Security Top 10](https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/).

This repo is a **FastAPI** JSON API. Prefer Pydantic validation, dependency injection, and reverse-proxy / middleware for transport security.

## Never trust user input

- Validate and sanitize all external input (body, query, headers, files, env)
- Prefer allowlists and Pydantic models (`Field`, `min_length`, enums) over ad-hoc checks
- Bound expensive fields (e.g. `transcription` length) before calling LLMs

## XSS

- Escape HTML when rendering user content; never mark untrusted data as safe
- Avoid injecting raw user strings into HTML/JS contexts
- Be careful with Markdown/HTML from LLMs if ever rendered in a browser
- For pure JSON APIs, still treat response fields as untrusted if a frontend will display them

## CSRF

- Protect cookie-based session state-changing requests (CSRF tokens / SameSite)
- For pure Bearer/API-key JSON APIs without cookies, CSRF risk is lower — do not add fake CSRF theater; do not introduce cookie auth without CSRF

## Injection (SQL / command / prompt)

- Use parameterized queries / ORM only; never f-string or concatenate SQL
- Avoid raw SQL unless parameters are bound; never interpolate user input
- Do not build shell commands from user input (`subprocess` with `shell=False` + arg lists)
- Treat LLM prompts as untrusted composition: do not concatenate secrets; keep system vs user roles clear

## Clickjacking / headers

- Prefer `X-Frame-Options: DENY` (or CSP `frame-ancestors`) unless embedding is required
- Enable `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, HSTS when serving HTTPS
- Set security headers at the reverse proxy or with Starlette middleware when not already handled at the edge

## HTTPS / cookies / Host

- Production behind HTTPS; redirect HTTP→HTTPS at the edge when possible
- Secure cookies: `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `SameSite` when using sessions
- Validate Host / configure trusted hosts; never trust raw `Host` for security decisions
- Be extremely careful with forwarded proto/host headers from proxies (`X-Forwarded-*`)

## Secrets & debug

- Never ship verbose exception bodies or debug stack traces in production
- Secrets (API keys, tokens) only via env / Settings / secret manager — never in git
- Rotate keys if exposed; fail closed when required secrets are missing at startup
- Do not log Authorization headers, API keys, or raw `.env`

## Uploads, auth & expensive endpoints

- Limit upload size/type; serve user content from a separate domain when possible
- Rate-limit auth and expensive endpoints (e.g. LLM calls)
- Authorize at the data layer (broken access control is app logic, not FastAPI magic)
- Prefer FastAPI `Depends()` for auth and shared guards

## OpenAPI / docs

- Keep `/docs` and `/openapi.json` useful in development; restrict or protect them in production if the API is public and sensitive
- Never put real secrets in OpenAPI examples

## Dependencies

- Keep FastAPI, Starlette, Uvicorn, and other deps patched; audit lockfiles in CI when practical
